00:01:20,298 --> 00:01:22,580
principles and laws which have a bigger

33
00:01:22,692 --> 00:01:25,343
impact on the cleanness of your code. And

34
00:01:25,343 --> 00:01:27,383
for that, we will start with the Law of

35
00:01:27,528 --> 00:01:28,010
Demeter.

36
00:01:28,973 --> 00:01:31,431
Now, what is the Law of Demeter? It is

37
00:01:31,527 --> 00:01:33,340
related to code like this.

38
00:01:34,415 --> 00:01:37,656
Here in this code, which could be in some

39
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:39,485
method of some class,

40
00:01:40,111 --> 00:01:42,501
we are accessing the customer property of

41
00:01:42,533 --> 00:01:43,127
the class,

42
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:47,074
on that property, the lastPurchase property

43
00:01:47,090 --> 00:01:47,715
as it seems,

44
00:01:48,293 --> 00:01:50,988
and on that lastPurchase property, which

45
00:01:51,036 --> 00:01:53,363
seems to be another object, we access the

46
00:01:53,507 --> 00:01:54,325
date property.

47
00:01:55,352 --> 00:01:57,983
You should avoid code like this.

48
00:01:58,802 --> 00:02:00,775
Drilling deeply into

49
00:02:01,368 --> 00:02:03,677
other objects is something which is

50
00:02:03,853 --> 00:02:05,633
discouraged by the Law of Demeter.

51
00:02:06,595 --> 00:02:08,455
Now, why is it discouraged?

52
00:02:09,225 --> 00:02:11,389
The Law of Demeter is in the end about the

53
00:02:11,389 --> 00:02:14,099
principle of least knowledge, which means

54
00:02:14,195 --> 00:02:17,658
your class shouldn't rely too much on the

55
00:02:17,658 --> 00:02:21,859
internals of strangers, of other objects you

56
00:02:22,052 --> 00:02:23,960
are interacting with in your code.

57
00:02:25,098 --> 00:02:27,760
In this example, customer would be a class

58
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:30,937
property, So interacting with it is fine and

59
00:02:30,985 --> 00:02:33,618
accessing lastPurchase is fine, but

60
00:02:33,618 --> 00:02:37,374
lastPurchase now is kind of a stranger. We

61
00:02:37,454 --> 00:02:40,680
access it indirectly through customer and we

62
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:42,382
shouldn't drill deeper into that.

63
00:02:43,088 --> 00:02:45,624
Now, why shouldn't we do that? Well, because

64
00:02:45,656 --> 00:02:47,663
whenever something changes about the

65
00:02:47,663 --> 00:02:51,194
internal structure of lastPurchase, all our

66
00:02:51,194 --> 00:02:54,084
code in this place where we have this line

67
00:02:54,084 --> 00:02:56,218
of code needs to change as well.

68
00:02:56,892 --> 00:02:59,056
And that then makes your code harder to

69
00:02:59,056 --> 00:03:02,391
extend and maintain because simple changes

70
00:03:02,727 --> 00:03:05,725
can result in a lot of changes in a lot of

71
00:03:05,789 --> 00:03:07,151
places of your application.

72
00:03:08,017 --> 00:03:10,390
And this law is closely related to our

73
00:03:10,582 --> 00:03:13,756
concept of writing clean code because for

74
00:03:13,788 --> 00:03:16,032
one, when writing clean code, we shouldn't

75
00:03:16,032 --> 00:03:17,058
repeat ourselves

76
00:03:17,635 --> 00:03:19,703
and we often might do that if we have

77
00:03:19,992 --> 00:03:20,921
statements like this.

78
00:03:22,043 --> 00:03:24,560
And in addition, statements like this are a

79
00:03:24,688 --> 00:03:27,510
bit harder to read. Analyzing such lines of

80
00:03:27,574 --> 00:03:30,085
code simply takes takes longer than short,

81
00:03:30,246 --> 00:03:31,423
simple statements.

82
00:03:32,343 --> 00:03:34,520
Of course, this isn't too difficult of a

83
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:36,633
statement, but it is also just a brief

84
00:03:36,633 --> 00:03:37,085
example.

85
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:40,845
So the Law of Demeter, which we should

86
00:03:40,893 --> 00:03:43,578
therefore also follow to achieve clean code,

87
00:03:44,031 --> 00:03:46,862
in the end states that code in a method

88
00:03:47,429 --> 00:03:50,482
may only access the direct internals, which

89
00:03:50,514 --> 00:03:51,936
would be properties and methods,

90
00:03:52,598 --> 00:03:55,262
of the object it belongs to, So you can

91
00:03:55,278 --> 00:03:58,088
access any property and method of the class

92
00:03:58,168 --> 00:03:59,870
of which this method in which you run the

93
00:03:59,870 --> 00:04:00,769
code is part of.

94
00:04:01,315 --> 00:04:03,644
You can access any objects which are stored

95
00:04:03,644 --> 00:04:05,651
in properties of that object. So in this

96
00:04:05,651 --> 00:04:08,605
case, accessing customer is fine because it

97
00:04:08,605 --> 00:04:11,479
is a property of the class this code belongs

