00:04:11,495 --> 00:04:13,727
to, as you can tell by the this keyword.

99
00:04:14,546 --> 00:04:17,147
And accessing lastPurchase is of course also

100
00:04:17,211 --> 00:04:20,727
fine because that directly is part of that

101
00:04:20,727 --> 00:04:21,833
customer property.

102
00:04:22,747 --> 00:04:24,975
You may also use any objects which you

103
00:04:24,975 --> 00:04:27,540
receive as method parameters. So it's not

104
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:29,656
just about properties of the class like

105
00:04:29,784 --> 00:04:32,333
customer, but if customer would be received

106
00:04:32,509 --> 00:04:35,218
as a parameter, we would also be allowed to

107
00:04:35,394 --> 00:04:36,452
access data in it.

108
00:04:37,158 --> 00:04:39,979
And of course, we also are allowed to

109
00:04:39,979 --> 00:04:42,223
interact with objects which are created in

110
00:04:42,223 --> 00:04:42,608
the method.

111
00:04:43,345 --> 00:04:45,878
So if we would instantiate another class, we

112
00:04:45,910 --> 00:04:48,218
can of course also work with the API and

113
00:04:48,315 --> 00:04:49,966
properties of that class.

114
00:04:50,543 --> 00:04:52,819
That's the idea behind the law of Dammiter.

115
00:04:53,669 --> 00:04:56,506
And here is this example from the slide now

116
00:04:56,714 --> 00:04:59,023
in a real dummy class.

